[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two simple, matte ceramic bowls set side-by-side on an aged oak wood tabletop, centered in the frame with generous negative space. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view at 40 degrees, ensuring every component of the salad is visible. The lighting is soft natural side light from a window to the left, creating gentle highlights on the vegetable surfaces and soft shadows. Shallow depth of field keeps the front bowl in sharp focus with the second bowl softly blurred. The salad consists of bright green, plump edamame kernels mixed with sweet golden corn and finely chopped red onion, tossed in a light, glistening olive oil and lime dressing. A subtle dusting of deep red chili powder coats the vegetables, and flecks of vibrant fresh green cilantro are distributed throughout. Props include a small wooden spoon resting casually against one bowl and a crumpled, off-white linen napkin placed slightly off-center. A few stray kernels of corn and a stray cilantro leaf are scattered on the wood surface near the bowl to signify the cooking process. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene looks like a home-cooked meal, warm and authentic, with slight natural imperfections like an uneven distribution of ingredients and a small droplet of lime dressing on the rim. No plastic sheen, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
